<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged by Tauri into the Info.plist of every macOS build, the setup's copy
  and the Mac App Store's alike: a file named Info.plist next to
  tauri.conf.json is picked up without being named in any config.

  What Tauri writes itself and is therefore not here: CFBundleIdentifier
  (app.uwussh.desktop), the versions, LSApplicationCategoryType (from
  bundle.category, DeveloperTool → public.app-category.developer-tools),
  LSMinimumSystemVersion, and CFBundleURLTypes for uwussh:// links (from
  plugins.deep-link).

  ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption is true, on purpose. UwUSSH implements SSH
  itself (russh on ring: ChaCha20-Poly1305, AES-GCM/CTR, Curve25519, ECDH,
  Ed25519, RSA), speaks TLS through rustls, and seals its vault with
  ChaCha20-Poly1305 under an Argon2 key — standard algorithms, but not the
  encryption macOS provides, and not limited to authentication. That is
  "non-exempt" in App Store Connect's terms; it fits the US mass-market rules
  (ECCN 5D992.c, EAR 740.17(b)(1), no report since 2021) and needs Apple's
  French encryption declaration if the app is offered in France. With the
  flag set, App Store Connect asks once per build until the approved
  documentation's code is added here as ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode.
  docs/app-store.md → Export compliance has the answers and what is open.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
